Z||000005||filthyscarecrow||03-07-2001||11:54 PM||||ya know, a lot of people claim to know why these kids do this stuff. school administrators htink that they're helping with their "zero-tolerance policies" adn it's all a bunch of crap. i wrote a paper on this for english one year and my research actually found that schools with 0-tloerance policies had MORE violence than schools with more leniant policies. maybe a reactance principle? but really, zero tolerance policies establish kids as trouble makers in the eyes of the administrators adn when there are real problems with those kids, the administrators treat them like lost causes. <BR>  i also hear tons of people saying that "being picked on in high school can't be bad enough to kill someone." well, that's a bunch of sheeit too. having been one of those kids i can tell you that i thouhgt about that stuff too. i would never have done it though because i'm not a violent person, but there were days when i feigned illness to get out of going to school becasue i couldn't take it. and the problem is that people picking on me had no idea what i was dealing with at home. they didn't care. there were people that i wouldn't have felt too bad about erasing. <BR>  then i got contacts, grew up bigger than most eveyone in my class, and smashed a kid's head through a bus window and people started to realise that their behavior was really affecting me. i hate violence, but that kid deserved it, and i didn't take it any farther than i had to to get him to leave me alone. and it worked. but with my knowledge of things that go boom, i could very easily have done in the whole school. i don't condone in the least what these kids have done, but i understand why they did it, and while murder is the worst crime one can commit, i feel more for them than for their victims. their victims may be dead, but they at least got to live in the first place. the kids that killed them never got to, and never will...<P>------------------<BR>allow me to show you my exhaust pipes...||||reg||1
